ps via correspondence with  yale faculty and students  some of these epistolary programs  included a discussion section on race  religion and american empire  by then phd candidate victoria baina  a reading group on black marxism by  professor ferguson and a creative  writing workshop organized by me and two  other student fellows  and through these remote engagements the  ypi students were able to take all of  the materials as starting points to  facilitate their own in-person seminars  and discussion groups during the  quarantine  in terms of the creative writing  workshop which was i was involved with  as a first year here in the phd program  i worked with two undergrad fellows  ananya and gabby who  really spearheaded the program  and we invited a broad range of writers  artists and academics across  the country to curate their own packets  of creative writing reflections readings  and response questions to send to the  students  some of these writers were on the last  slide but they included  frannie choi chen chen  alexander chi brian hopper tavia nyong'o  and after the students received the  packets they responded to some of the  suggested prompts and sent back pieces  of poetry nonfiction fiction memoir and  other creative writing texts  to be workshopped by uh student editors  here at yale  and even some of the pieces ended up  being published on some on campus uh  on-campus publications that i'll show  later  um i won't bog you down with too many  more of these details about the contents  or logistics but you can visit the  website at the top to learn more  [Music]  but  yeah and now with classes in person  there are versions of the workshop that  are running  in the fall as well  so then sort of moving away from  information and instead towards  reflection i just wanted to share these  two quotes from two scholars who have  thought about the necessity of art and  of letter writing as genres of intimacy  that facilitate alternative conceptions  of space of shared study and of  community formation  in the top left nicole fleetwood in her  latest book marking time writes quote  art and by art she means of all mediums  creates relational possibilities that  disrupt the mandate of the prison such  artistic practices radically challenged  the impenetrability of prison  in the bottom right orasami burton  writes that letter writing within and  beyond prisons functions as a form of  community building grassroots  intelligence gathering collective  theorization and mutual aid  so resonant in these two quotes that i  just wanted to share  is this idea of slipperiness and how art  and letter writing are collective  practices that trouble  the architectures of borders walls and  locks  and throughout the letter exchanges  during the pandemic within ypei you know  there's this sort of there was like this  sort of anxiety about um  but still like a reassurance about um  the uncertainties that snail mail  presents so one of the barriers that we  faced when adapting to the pandemic was  that sending and receiving physical  letters took up to two weeks  given travel time oversight and  processing within the prison  but and this delay and timing basically  meant that there was a sort of  disjuncture between sender and receiver  because in new haven we'd have to work  proactively well in advance whereas the  students in suffield  had to work more retroactively given the  delays and you know and when letters are  sent there's this kind of like ambiguous  um  sense of like you know did it get lost  in the mail uh will the c will the  receiver reply when will i write back um  and despite these logistical and  emotional anxieties which were  compounded by the pandemic and its  mandate for like separation and  quarantining the ypi community was still  able to be in an intimate network of  letter exchanges for around a year  um  and lastly in these two quotes fleetwood  and burton you know are speaking  specifically about art and letter  writing in the context of the prison but  i would even extend their analysis to  the university too you know as these  letters were crucial modes of community  even for those here in new haven um and  our shift to um  purely zoom and then  so moving  on to the quite recent uh in april 2021  ypi received a three-year grant from the  andrew mellon foundation  and this 1.5 million dollar grant has  been really pivotal  in allowing ypi students both current  and prospective to earn a two-year  associate's degrees from the university  of new haven by taking courses taught by  both yale and unh faculty  and grad students  so again we see this sort of like  increasing slipperiness between the  university and the prison  as faculty and graduate students across  colleges begin collaborating and as  incarcerated students begin acquiring  degrees that were and i would argue  are still reserved um for the  unincarcerated populations  and since this mellon grant ypi has been  able to offer  these in-person courses  this past summer and this ongoing fall  through partnerships with yale and the  university of new haven so in addition  to the creative writing workshop uh this  first course on the left  english 120 was my first was my next  main engagement with ypei  um but this time it was in a more  official role as a teaching fellow  funded through ypi's partnership with  yale summer session  and this transition from  undergraduate student volunteer to  graduate student teacher has been really  formative for me  in terms of reshaping my commitments and  also uncommitments to pedagogy  and higher education as they are  structured the way they are now  um so this quote here a real  togetherness was developing  is from angela davis's autobiography  where she reflects on the necessity  of a pedagogy  um while recounting her time being  incarcerated in the new york woman's  house of detention but still continuing  her professorial work but through the  lens of a jailhouse pedagogy  and the reason why i invoke this quote  this idea of togetherness as an active  and ongoing verb  is because  the english 120 creative writing course  which is one of the first classes the  new cohort of ypi students took and one  of the intro classes that a lot of yale  first years take was not just a standard  seminar discussion but also had peer  review workshops at least once a week  and as someone who took creative writing  workshops here as an undergrad i noticed  that the sort of practice of community  took on extremely different shapes  between the classrooms at softfield  versus in new haven  um so for the ypi students you know  togetherness manifests  as a form of like shared precarity and  collective study and struggle  and workshops were at spaces of honest  and kind but also rigorous and difficult  conversations you know whereas classes  at yale  and new haven i think sometimes have  tinges of  professionalization socialization and  therefore some shieldedness that i think  can get in the way of learning together  in a collective and vulnerable fashion  um and just in sharing this anecdote um  i just wanted to index that teaching in  ypei has allowed me to garner not just a  wonderful community of friends and  interlocutors but also a set of um  they're not tools but like more so like  principles like guiding principles to  bring into the classes here at yale  buildings as i continue to teach and  enter my third and fourth years  um so i just wanted to give  three quotes that we can put in  conversation with each other um all from  different perspectives so this quote  here is um  a ypei student testimony i'll just read  bits of it  i've taken strides educationally that  were once unfathomable let alone  unreachable unreachable not only because  of accessibility but because of the  mental emotional social and intellectual  stand still  my brain  my brain and heart have been stored in  that ypi has unlocked the dean's  professors students former students and  volunteers that i've been blessed to  come into contact with have treated me  with inequality that has been foreign to  me since my departure from the free  world  this is a reflection from a graduate  student instructor  they say i have grown increasingly  convinced that this initiative embodies  the very best of what yellows is and  inspires to be  a center of innovative teaching and  research that seeks to educate and learn  from the brightest most creative  students wherever they may be  and then this third  slide is a reflection from a ypi faculty  member rod ferguson and he writes that  um  uh one of the standout  stan singular teaching experiences for  him has been the opportunity to teach in  the yale prison education initiative as  students ypi students have  exacting standards as readers and  interlocutors they're not just going to  accept anything that you say because  you're the authority or because the text  was written by so and so they're also  incredibly generous  so in giving you these three faces of  ypei  i think they all prompt a sort of  revisitation and also a reconsideration  of the purpose of higher education and  our own roles within it as members of  the university um  so here's yale college's mission  statement and i just underlined some  points some phrases that i think  could be further  discussed um  and yeah i think  the yale college mission statement in  context of  the young person education initiative  but also higher ed  uh in prison programs beyond um  prompt us to think about you know who  is excluded but also included from this  mission statement and what sort of  possibilities lie in the future  [Music]  and yes these are just some slides of  the ypi students collaborations with  on-campus publications during the  creative writing workshop on the left is  the spring 2020 issue of the litmag  where we had some students publish their  poetry alongside  el students here in new haven  in the middle is a ydn article  it was a special issue where they  invited ypi students to  send some pieces related to their  experiences in ypei and on the right is  this awesome  multimedia project that one of our  students had uh did with the beineckey  our book and manuscript library and they  published a sort of mini exhibit with  writing and drawings  um yeah so in terms of  opportunities and ways to get involved  you can learn more about it at the the  fair next door but  some partnerships for student support  together we work with the porvu center  for teaching and learning to bring in  academic strategies mentors and writing  partners to the students  we also work with the yale university  library  sort of bring archival and textbook  resources to students as they do their  research papers and final projects  there's a  [Music]  yul and ypei student coordinator  position that will be opening next  spring and it's a paid student position  and it's ideally for a grad student  interested in library services and  programming  and yes tfs and accessibility resources  as well  and here are some  what are they called qr codes that you  can scan if you're interested in  volunteering but yeah thank you so much  and happy to answer any questions  [Applause]  throughout the pandemic than with letter  writing i think the academic calendar is  such an interesting take on time has  that impacted at all or have you thought  at all about  how  the university might function  differently if we thought about time  differently  with the possibilities that you've been  able to do  now thinking about time  yeah that's a really helpful  question and i yeah i think the way time  operates for a classroom at mcdougall  walker versus time at a classroom here  in a yale building um is really  different i think  time  at mcdougall is really quick and it's um  there are a lot more logistical issues  that come with it so for example if a  student wants to write a research paper  that combines archival sources from a  yale library 